{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-114335",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since we have an overall common factor of $3$, we start by dividing our equation through by $3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}-3t+15=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}-3t+15 & = \\\\ ({t}^{3}-5{t}^{2})-(3t-15) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-5)-3(t-5) & = \\\\ (t-5)\\cdot {t}^{2}-(t-5)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (t-5)({t}^{2}-3) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(t-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-5 & =0 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({t}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $5$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
